WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.089
Imagine the government owns your city's bus company. One day it sells the company to private investors. Now the buses are run by a private firm that wants to make a profit.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:27.224
Privatisation is the sale or transfer of a government-owned firm to private investors. It moves from the public sector to the private sector. The opposite is nationalisation.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:37.086
Privatisation affects many stakeholders: the government, the firm, consumers, workers and rival firms. Each is affected differently.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:01:02.445
Step one, define privatisation. Step two, pick the stakeholder named in the question. The government gets sale revenue but loses control. The firm gets investment and freedom. Consumers may get lower prices, or face a private monopoly. Workers may lose jobs.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:13.148
Step three, explain the effect through the profit motive. New owners want profit, so they cut waste and invest, so efficiency rises.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:24.302
Step four, use the case. For nine- and twelve-mark answers, weigh the gains against risks such as a private monopoly, job cuts or price rises, then judge.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:45.512
Here is a real question from June 2024. In 2023, Portugal's government-owned airline, TAP Air, looked for investors so it could be privatised. Analyse the possible advantages of privatisation for TAP Air. Six marks.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:57.915
Define it. Privatisation means selling a government-owned firm to private investors. TAP Air would move from the Portuguese Government to private owners.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:11.190
Advantage one, investment. Investors put in new money, so TAP Air can buy newer aircraft or open new routes. It sells more flights and its revenue rises.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:24.873
Advantage two, the profit motive. The owners cut waste and plan schedules better, so the cost of each flight falls. TAP Air can then earn more profit or charge lower fares.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:40.291
Advantage three, freedom. With no government owner, decisions are not slowed by bureaucracy or changed for political reasons, so managers react quickly. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:52.584
A common mistake is defining privatisation as a firm becoming a private limited company. It means moving from government to private ownership.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:59.367
Another is claiming prices must fall. A private monopoly could raise them.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:15.373
Now you try. A government sells its state-owned electricity company to private investors. Explain one possible effect on the company's workers. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:29.972
The new owners want profit, so they look for ways to cut costs. Some jobs may be cut, or pay held down, so some workers lose income or job security.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:40.094
Remember: define privatisation, pick the stakeholder, explain through the profit motive, and use the case. Now try the practice questions.
